# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Aurelia line-ending policy - enforced by git, not by convention.
#
# Why this file exists
# -------------------
# Run https://github.com/ankworks/aurelia/actions/runs/38108794402 failed on the
# windows-2025 runner at the "patch-verification" stage with:
#
#   unexpected content before first file header:
#   "diff --git a/chrome/browser/BUILD.gn b/chrome/browser/BUILD.gn\r"
#
# The checked-out copy of chromium/patches/0001-aurelia-webui-and-resources.patch
# had CRLF line endings. Nothing in this repository told git otherwise, and the
# Git for Windows install on GitHub-hosted Windows runners leaves the installer
# default `core.autocrlf=true` in the SYSTEM gitconfig (the image passes
# /o:PathOption=CmdTools but no /o:AutoCRLF=false), so `actions/checkout`
# materialised every text file with CRLF.
#
# A digest-verified patch cannot survive that. The patch is hashed over its raw
# bytes, and `lib/patch.mjs` matches `diff --git` headers with a `$`-anchored
# regex that cannot match a line ending in CR (`\r` is an ECMAScript
# LineTerminator, so `.` never consumes it and `$` only matches end of input).
# .editorconfig already asked for `end_of_line = lf`, but EditorConfig is a
# convention that git does not consult on checkout. These rules are the part
# that actually binds.
#
# Chromium solves the same problem the same way: its own .gitattributes pins
# `*.cc text eol=lf`, `*.gn text eol=lf`, `*.py text eol=lf`, and so on.
#
# Rules
# -----
# 1. Text files always land in the working tree with LF, on every platform,
#    whatever core.autocrlf says.
# 2. Digest-verified artifacts are never converted at all - not on checkout and
#    not on `git add`. `-text` wins over the `*` rule below (git applies the
#    last matching pattern), so the bytes on disk are always the bytes that were
#    hashed. `tools/ci/check-line-endings.mjs` fails the build if this stops
#    being true.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* text=auto eol=lf

# Patch files are hashed (chromium/patches/*.meta.json -> sha256) and re-applied
# byte-for-byte with `git apply`. Never let git touch their bytes.
*.patch -text
*.diff -text
